WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.234
Former President Donald Trump

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.702
kicked off his 2024

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.804
campaign yesterday with a pair of events

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:05.805
in two

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:06.806
crucial early

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:07.907
voting states, South

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.943
Carolina and New Hampshire.

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:10.977
But these weren't large,

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.145
rambunctious rallies

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:13.113
that the former president

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:14.781
is known for a new approach

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:15.949
approach, perhaps,

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:17.617
as the Republican Party considers

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.285
whether it needs a new face

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:20.520
at the top of the ticket.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.054
Here with me

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:22.355
now is Republican Governor

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.391
Chris Sununu of New Hampshire.

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:26.059
Thank you so much for joining me.

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:27.494
I want to talk politics in a second,

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.362
but first I want to ask about

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.630
what happened in Memphis

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.599
and the type of Nichols

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.000
the video that we have seen

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.669
and most importantly, the brutal beating.

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:37.370
Deadly beating.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.406
I mean, I think first when I saw it

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:41.408
you see it as I see it as a dad. Right.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:42.242
And what that father

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:42.942
must have been going through

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:43.543
watching that.

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.112
I mean, it's just so gut wrenching.

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:46.713
But then obviously,

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.181
I do have to transition, I think.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:48.615
Okay, well, I'm

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:49.682
the governor of the state.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.351
I oversee police standards and training

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:52.052
by obviously state

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.019
police and law enforcement.

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:54.621
What are we doing it at our level?

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.289
And so you want to make sure that those

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:57.791
there's a sense of accountability

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:59.259
and it's not just training of course.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.794
I mean, what you saw there was beyond

00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:02.695
a training failure was humanity.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.564
And I think everyone acknowledges that.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.099
So, again,

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:06.766
you got to make sure

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:07.600
you're being transparent.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.802
We want to make sure for example,

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:09.803
all our law enforcement

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:10.970
officers are seeing that video,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.739
understanding what what happened, what

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:14.274
where those failures really were.

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:16.109
And they clearly go beyond the video,

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:17.210
unfortunately. Right.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:17.410
You know,

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.878
what is inherent within our system.

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.013
So I think everyone across

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.081
the country is taking a look at it.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:21.915
But that's that's

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.516
the kind of the tough part of leadership

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:24.250
in terms of making sure

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.452
that you're driving forward with,

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:27.053
you know, the right process going forward

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:28.121
in the right mentality.

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:28.621
With your lead,

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:29.756
with your teams on the ground.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.458
A lot of calls, renewed calls

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:33.693
for Congress to deal with

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:35.662
comprehensive police reform.

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:36.362
New Hampshire,

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.064
your state, is famously

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.233
a libertarian, live free or die.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:40.867
Do you believe

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:41.734
the federal government

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.637
should take the lead in having a uniform,

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.207
so to speak, a

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:47.974
set of rules

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.809
and regulations on a national level?

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:50.810
I don't mind the federal government

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:51.678
creating guidelines,

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:52.445
but let's understand

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:53.913
what's what's important

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:54.747
and what the issues are in

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:55.682
in New York City

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:57.183
or in Boston or in New Hampshire,

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.118
earner in Arizona,

00:01:58.118 --> 00:01:59.519
might be all very, very different.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:00.520
What how we try

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.688
how we train is very different.

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:02.155
In New Hampshire,

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:04.157
we have one centralized training facility

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:05.792
for local and state enforcement.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:06.659
So it allows us

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.161
when we change one of our processes,

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:08.995
it makes sure that it goes

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:09.896
through the whole system.

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:11.397
Not every state does it that way.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.867
So I think the guidelines are okay,

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:14.367
making sure

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:16.102
that certain issues of mental

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:17.871
health and mental illness

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.539
of safety, of officers, of,

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:20.707
you know, issues of bail reform

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:21.674
and all of these sort of things,

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:22.742
they can provide those guidelines.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.377
But I do believe that states and cities

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:24.777
and towns

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:25.311
really need to make

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.013
a lot of those decisions on their own.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.681
Let's talk a little politics.

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:29.516
You're here.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:29.883
Is it

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:30.717
because Donald Trump

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:32.018
was in New Hampshire?

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:32.986
No, no.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:34.988
I heard he was I hadn't made my plans.

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:36.723
And then you said, let's get out of here.

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:37.323
Oh, heck no.

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:39.259
But one of the reasons he

00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:41.928
became the Republican nominee in 2016

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:43.496
is because

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.331
he won the first primary states.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.566
Your state of New Hampshire

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.468
Could he do that again? Oh, he could.

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:49.569
I don't think he will.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:50.170
He could.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:50.737
You know, one thing

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:51.871
I mean, obviously, he's

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:53.239
he started his campaign

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:55.008
after the election in 22

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:55.909
that obviously

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:57.744
had a lot of stumbling blocks

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.012
that he was trying to overcome.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.213
He comes to New Hampshire

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:02.682
and frankly gives a very mundane speech.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.050
The response he received

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.351
as he read his teleprompter,

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.853
he stuck to the talking points.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:07.420
He went away.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.955
So he's not really bringing that fire,

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:09.689
that energy.

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:11.491
I think that a lot of folks saw it in 16.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:12.559
I think in many ways

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:13.459
it was a little disappointing

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.093
to some folks.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:14.994
And even he acknowledged

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:16.229
it wasn't his best.

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:17.997
He came to a predetermined crowd,

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.132
not one of his big rallies.

00:03:19.132 --> 00:03:20.166
And so

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:22.068
I think a lot of folks understand that,

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.636
you know, he's going to be a candidate,

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:24.771
but he's also going to have to earn it.

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:25.638
And that's New Hampshire

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.040
even if you're the former president,

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:27.807
you've got to come

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:28.908
and earn it, person or person.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:29.943
I want to play something

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:31.644
that you said in your inaugural

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:32.845
address earlier this month.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:35.748
It's not

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:37.116
right to tell a private business

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.851
what they can or cannot do.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.320
It is not the New Hampshire way

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.688
to force locally elected

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.189
and accountable school districts

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.324
or town councils

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:46.292
to bend to the State House's will.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.193
That's not leadership

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:48.361
and it's not conservative

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.797
and it is certainly not freedom.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.165
If I didn't know better,

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.467
I would think that that was a

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.736
thinly veiled shot at your fellow

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.871
Republican governor down south.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:00.006
Governor Ron DeSantis,

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:01.374
because he's made a point

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:02.275
of picking fights

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:03.910
with private businesses and schools

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.578
over cultural issues.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:05.878
Well, look,

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:06.946
I think a lot of Republican

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:08.448
leadership is getting behind

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:09.682
this idea that we have to fight.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:10.483
And I get it,

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:11.884
as in a leadership position,

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.853
you have to be willing to have the fight

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.221
But we cannot have leadership.

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:17.056
That is only about the fight.

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:18.524
I'm a I'm a free market.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:20.260
Who is who does

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.361
show that they are leading

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:22.228
only about the fight?

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:23.463
Who were you referring to?

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.597
Oh, I don't think it's any individual.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:25.064
I think, look,

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:26.733
a lot of Republicans are frustrated,

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:27.433
a lot of conservatives,

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:28.167
a lot of Americans,

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:29.736
independents are frustrated

00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:30.803
because they see this

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:31.971
culture, this woke

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.773
cancel culture, invading

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:35.708
our societies and in our communities

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:37.377
and we need to fight. You argue that.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:37.543
Yeah,

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:38.344
but you argue that

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.579
there's too much of that.

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:40.380
There is that.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:40.680
There's not

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.449
there's too much of the focus on fighting

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:44.751
My argument is

00:04:44.751 --> 00:04:46.519
the government is not the solution

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:47.487
to cultural issues

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:48.621
and we have a cultural problem,

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:49.689
but the government

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:50.423
isn't going to come down

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:51.691
with a big, heavy hammer

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:52.992
and solve that problem.

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:54.894
As Ron DeSantis doing that too much.

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:55.862
It's not about wrong.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.230
I think I think the Republican Party

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:57.764
as a whole

00:04:57.764 --> 00:04:58.865
just wants to get out and fight

00:04:58.865 --> 00:04:59.999
as opposed to saying, look,

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:00.533
let's remember

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:01.801
we're about limited government,

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:02.969
local control,

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:04.804
believing in individual responsibility.

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:05.938
That's that's the Live Free

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:06.839
or die spirit in New Hampshire.

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:07.840
And the model works really well.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.075
University of New Hampshire

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:10.376
released a new poll this week.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:11.311
You might have seen it

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:13.946
potential GOP 2024 candidates.

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:15.648
We'll put it up on the screen.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.417
Ron DeSantis is doing quite well

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:18.351
over the former president.

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:19.252
You are on there.

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.121
Speaking of that,

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:23.523
when will you make a decision

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:24.324
one way or another

00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:25.992
whether you will run for president?

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:26.793
I don't have a timeline.

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:28.161
I mean obviously folks are talking about

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:29.462
but I really don't have a timeline.

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:30.797
I was getting a lot of time nationally

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:31.264
trying to grow

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.132
the party as Republicans

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:34.067
talk to independents,

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:35.568
talk to the next generation

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:36.869
of potential Republican voters

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:37.403
that right now

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:39.072
no one is really reaching out to.

00:05:39.072 --> 00:05:40.073
But I think we have a lot of hope

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:40.673
and optimism

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:41.908
in a way to kind of

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:43.109
want them to be on our team

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.310
and they should want to be on our team.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:45.678
It's not just about the negativity

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:46.946
that you see in the media and the press

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:47.580
and that they've

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.781
kind of been inundated with.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.750
It really is about good government.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:51.451
And I'm trying to

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:51.951
I think we've done

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:52.985
that really well in New Hampshire,

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:53.553
trying to bring that

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:54.520
a little bit to the table

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.288
and then down the road

00:05:55.288 --> 00:05:56.556
if that leads into something bigger.

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:57.857
So you're considering it? Yes.

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.226
When do

00:06:00.226 --> 00:06:01.627
other people, whether it's

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.596
you or anybody else, need to get in

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:05.698
because he's the only declared candidate?

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.099
Well, yes.

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:07.333
I mean, obviously, DeSantis and Trump

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:08.501
are the two candidates. Right.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:09.969
So whether he has cleared or not.

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:11.904
Well, he's not declared

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:12.572
okay.

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:13.506
Not publicly declared,

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:14.507
but he has hundreds of millions

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:15.208
of dollars.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:15.775
Obviously, Ron

00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:18.111
DeSantis is looking to run for president,

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:18.678
which is fine.

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:19.612
And he'll probably win

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:21.347
New Hampshire right now, without a doubt.

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:22.315
I think other candidates

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:23.716
will look to get in this spring

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.051
or into the summer.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:26.452
I'm surprised other candidates

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:27.887
I think a lot of us aren't doing better.

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:28.554
Right.

00:06:28.688 --> 00:06:30.156
And there's great candidates,

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:30.790
Mike Pence

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:31.457
and Nikki Haley

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:33.693
and Mike Pence wasn't even on the list.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:35.094
Yeah, I don't

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.295
I didn't see the poll, to be honest.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:37.930
But but no, I'm surprised

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:38.731
some of those aren't.

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:39.632
So the think

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:41.167
that you all you

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.101
who are considering

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:43.603
and I'm putting you in that category

00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:44.170
because you just said

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.605
you are aren't doing better

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:48.207
because you're not more forward leaning.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:49.142
Because you're not.

00:06:49.142 --> 00:06:49.709
Oh, yeah.

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:50.243
I'm surprised.

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:51.778
I'm on I'm on that pull it all, frankly.

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:52.779
But because we haven't

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.781
really been been talking about that,

00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:55.982
I think there's there's

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:57.517
a lot of hope, an opportunity

00:06:57.517 --> 00:06:59.318
for good things to get in and drive

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:00.720
the message where it needs to be

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:01.854
but the discipline is getting out

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:02.889
to the discipline of saying,

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:04.090
look, you're only pulling a 5%.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:04.724
You got to get out.

00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:06.392
We don't want a crowded field here.

00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:06.726
And that's

00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:07.760
kind of the referee of the

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.362
first in the nation primary.

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:10.296
I'll have some opportunity

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:11.431
there to pull some levers

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:13.132
and really make sure that we're holding

00:07:13.132 --> 00:07:14.400
the candidates accountable,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.435
make a good run at it.

00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:16.602
But if it ain't happening,

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:17.537
you got to pull out

00:07:17.537 --> 00:07:18.504
and leave it down to the two,

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:19.038
three or four.

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:20.239
And we'll talk another time

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:21.073
about what happens

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.109
when the referee is in the game.

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:24.310
If that happens for you.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.645
That's happened in South Carolina.

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:26.679
That'll happen in Florida.

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:27.647
That's true, right?

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:27.947
Last time I

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:29.248
check Ron's to go from Florida,

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:29.749
but I'm

00:07:29.749 --> 00:07:30.950
what I'm talking about you, Governor.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:31.818
Thank you so much.

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:32.819
Thank you. Appreciate it.

